<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chennakeshava Temple, Belur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1117</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chennakeshava temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, consecrated in 1117 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a>. Built of soapstone on a star-shaped platform, it is covered with finely carved friezes, bracket figures and sculpture. It is one of the finest examples of Hoysala architecture and part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chennakeshava-temple-belur.jpg" alt="Chennakeshava Temple, Belur" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bikashrd · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Courtyard_of_Chennakesava_Temple_-_Belur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a>, called Beluhur, Velur or Velapura in old inscriptions, stands on the Yagachi River and was an early capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> kings, who called it an earthly Vaikuntha, the abode of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. Vishnuvardhana, who came to power in 1110, commissioned the temple to the god Vijayanarayana; the scholar Dhaky sees in it his devotion to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vaishnavism">Sri Vaishnavism</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanujacharya</a>. His queen Santala Devi sponsored the smaller Chennigaraya temple beside it at the same time. The complex continued to grow for more than a century, and the temple has remained in worship ever since, unlike the other great Hoysala temples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The main temple is a single shrine of soapstone, which is soft when quarried and hardens with time, set on a jagati platform for walking around the sanctum. It combines North Indian Nagara and South Indian Karnata elements and drew on the Western Chalukyan tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pattadakal">Pattadakal</a>. Bands of carving run round the walls: elephants at the base, then horsemen, dancers and musicians, scenes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, and scenes of daily life, courtship and festivals. There are 80 large reliefs of deities, 38 surviving madanakai bracket figures, and a Jina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> among the images.</p><p>Inside, the navaranga hall, the largest of any <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> temple, has forty eight pillars, among them the Narasimha and Mohini pillars, under a domed ceiling about 10 feet across. The sanctum holds a four-armed image of Keshava about 6 feet high, its halo carved with the ten avatars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. Artists signed some of their work: Ruvari Mallitamma is credited with more than 40 sculptures, and Dasoja and his son Chavana with several madanikas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> armies plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a> in the 14th century, and a raid under the general Salar, serving Muhammed bin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq</a>, burnt the gateway. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> repaired the temple under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harihara-ii">Harihara II</a>, adding four granite pillars in 1381 and a seven-storey gopuram in 1397. Further repairs followed in 1709 and 1774. In the late 19th century the collapsing tower over the sanctum was removed and never replaced, and in 1935 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> government restored parts of the complex. In 2023 it became part of the Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas World Heritage Site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chennakeshava Temple, Belur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
